WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.208
- Law school is bull (beep).

00:00:02.208 --> 00:00:05.375
(plucky string music)

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.280
Hey, Legal Eagles, D. James Stone here

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.090
teaching you how to think like a lawyer

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:12.890
so you can crush law school.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:15.050
Now law school isn't really bull (beep),

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:18.560
but there is one part of it
that is a total bait and switch

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.480
that you might not know if you're heading

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:21.930
to law school for the first time

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.750
and you might not even really realize that

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.300
if you're halfway through it,

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.960
and if there's one thing I want you

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.583
to remember about law school, it's this,

00:00:29.583 --> 00:00:32.520
only the final matters.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.120
There's so much crap in law school

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.840
that makes difference
for your final grades.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.600
So don't get bogged down in things

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.911
that just don't translate into grades

00:00:41.911 --> 00:00:44.500
at the end of the semester,
and that's things like,

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.070
case briefs, cold calls in class,

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:49.890
and sometimes study groups.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.640
For most law school classes the only thing

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.875
that effects your grade at
the end of the semester,

00:00:54.875 --> 00:00:57.140
is your final exam.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.240
For most classes, and sure there are some

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.930
exceptions to this rule,
the midterm doesn't count,

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:03.850
class participation doesn't count,

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.570
going to office hours with
a professor doesn't count,

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.460
doing killer case briefs doesn't count,

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.700
it just doesn't matter and
the only thing that does

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.520
is your final exam at
the end of the semester,

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.080
and as a result, that can wreak havoc

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:18.780
on what law students do.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:22.590
Law students tend to be
super A type personalities,

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:25.367
who are used to having weigh
points throughout the semester.

00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:27.700
Tasks that will result in a grade

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:28.900
to know if they're on track.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.520
They'll have essays,
they'll have book reports,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.528
they'll have midterms,
all of these things result

00:01:34.528 --> 00:01:37.210
in getting grades along the way

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:39.253
and it seems like that linear progression

00:01:39.253 --> 00:01:42.120
results in a good grade at
the end of the semester,

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.000
but in law school, they
flip that on it's head.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.900
The only thing that matters is the final.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.830
So the most important thing
that you can do in law school,

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:53.600
is focus on the things
that help on the final,

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.589
and those are things like practice exams,

00:01:55.589 --> 00:01:59.880
learning the strategies
for an issue spotting exam,

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.900
and by the way you can get our checklist

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.120
for final exams in the links below.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.380
There's so much stuff during the semester

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:10.648
that seems incredibly important,
reading every single case,

00:02:10.648 --> 00:02:14.190
front to back, multiple
times, doing case briefs,

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.480
and law students tend to be programmed

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.530
into thinking that this
linear progression of things

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.620
is actually helping them
to achieve their end goal,

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.960
but in law school that
is simply not the case,

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.210
there is just so much
stuff that you can do,

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.850
and everyone has the
exact same amount of time.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:33.303
So the war is won and lost
on being able to focus on

00:02:33.303 --> 00:02:37.020
exactly the things that actually do help.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.520
If you're faced with a
task during law school,

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.370
the question you should ask yourself is,

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:44.060
will this help me on the final exam?

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.520
If it doesn't, you should
consider replacing it

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:49.120
with something that will
help on the final exam.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.710
Those are things like practice exams.

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:52.440
Practice exams are one of the best things

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.741
that you can possibly
do to learn how to ace

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:57.510
an issue spotting exam at
the end of the semester.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.289
It's things like learning the strategies

00:02:59.289 --> 00:03:01.410
for writing an issue spotting exam.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:03.780
I bet that most people go into law school

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.980
without any idea of the strategies

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:09.743
that will actually help them
write a law specific essay.

00:03:09.743 --> 00:03:11.621
Here's a newsflash, all of those essays

00:03:11.621 --> 00:03:13.920
that you wrote in English class,

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.473
or for other classes in
college and undergrad,

00:03:16.473 --> 00:03:18.681
those won't translate into writing

00:03:18.681 --> 00:03:22.710
a law school specific issue spotting exam.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:24.150
So those are just a handful of things

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:25.408
that you can do.

00:03:25.408 --> 00:03:29.410
There's a quote that I love,
by the Greek poet, Archilochus.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:32.477
It says, "We don't rise to
the level of our expectations,

00:03:32.477 --> 00:03:34.690
"we fall to the level of our training."

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:36.710
And so many people go into law school

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:39.730
thinking what got them
here will get them there,

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:41.390
and that's just not the case.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.040
Law school is a different animal

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.793
and it's too easy to get bogged down

00:03:45.793 --> 00:03:47.110
with things that take up so much time,

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:49.490
but have zero return on investment.

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:51.340
And believe me, I'm no different.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:55.040
When I was 1L and about
halfway through my semester,

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.500
I was preparing for being cold call.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.180
The next day the professor
said that I was the one

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:01.500
that was going to be on the hot seat,

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.220
and I was gonna be
answering the questions.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:04.820
So I prepared like crazy,

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:06.820
I read until three o'clock in the morning

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.280
and it occurred to me at about
three o'clock in the morning

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.315
that all of this information
that I was studying,

00:04:11.315 --> 00:04:14.115
this minute detail, this minutiae

00:04:14.115 --> 00:04:16.880
was not going to help
me on the final exam.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.240
I was terrified of
being called on in class

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.140
by the professor and looking like a fool

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.730
in front of my classmates,
but the truth is,

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:25.990
I had read a couple of
issue spotting essays,

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:28.440
and I knew that they just don't

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:31.640
require you to know that kind of detail.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.460
They require analysis and application,

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.510
and learning that detail for class

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:39.770
wasn't going to help me
learn how to analyze things

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:41.180
like and actual attorney.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.530
So that's when I learned
that I needed to focus

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.650
on learning those skills
instead of focusing

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:48.170
on the minutiae of things in class.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:49.980
So I got through that day on the hot seat,

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:53.220
and it was fine, the professor was a jerk,

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:56.700
and I didn't especially
enjoy it, but I ignored it.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.840
I knew that, that temporary stress was

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:02.510
going to give way to better
grades on the final exam,

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:04.950
because that was the thing
that I was going to focus on,

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:06.840
and you can do the same thing.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:08.710
It's a total mindset shift.

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:12.610
You can make your life
easier and gt better grades

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:15.820
at the same time by studying
smarter and not harder,

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:19.020
and focusing on the most
important thing to know

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:23.080
about law school, and that's
only the final matters.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.190
So that is the one thing
that I would impart

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:27.290
to new law students out there.

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:29.300
IF you wanna know more,
and if you like this video,

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.020
please check out this playlist of videos

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:33.803
that are guaranteed to
help you crush law school.

00:05:34.971 --> 00:05:37.263
So click on the playlist, and
I'll see you I the next video.

